Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Rusty Refractor Swallows Mars
- Story & Context: At 02:40 AM outside Estación Ramos Mejía, Joaquín dropped his battery synth into an empty yerba mate gourd to amplify its harsh square-wave chirp while the train's steel doors shuddered to a brutal 3:2 dembow. When an undercover transit guard tried to seize the auxiliary splitter chained across three seat rows, forty insomniac riders erupted into a clanging riot of kicked tambourines and dropped safety whistles, spilling out onto the ballast. Escaping up a warehouse fire escape with a dented brass refractor telescope scavenged from a swap meet, they pointed the lens past the diesel soot to Mars and screamed into a megaphone: "Baja la vista que el cielo se quema!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: High-contrast film photography of an amateur brass telescope aimed from a gravel rooftop into red-tinged industrial smog, surrounded by discarded pocket synthesizers, copper cables, and a taped tambourine on the tar paper.

Track 2: “Turn That Monotron Toward the Zenith”
- Story & Context: Crouched on a cracked brick parapet overlooking the rail yards, Lucía tested a dying nine-volt battery with the tip of her tongue before jamming it into a ribbon synth taped to her telescope tripod. Tar from the tenement roof blackened the knees of her torn fishnets as she confessed to the crew that she had pawned her sister's train pass just to buy the mixer they were using on the night shuttle. When a police helicopter spotlight swept the gravel and washed out their view of the Crab Nebula, she hammered her tambourine against a rusty exhaust vent and spat into the mic: "No busco estrellas, busco el choque!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Gritty cinematic shot looking up from a roof hatch at an insomniac girl adjusting a tiny synthesizer taped to a telescope mount, backlit by harsh searchlights and the glare of elevated city tracks.

Track 3: Desvelo de Catenaria
- Story & Context: When a wildcat transit strike halted every locomotive across the southern sector, the riders turned the empty Talleres rail depot into an open-air fortress, insisting the ghost dembow could still be heard vibrating through the overhead electric wires. A grease-stained apprentice known as El Mudo rallied seventy sleepless commuters along the dead tracks with battery amps strapped to handcarts, their coveralls reeking of brake fluid and sulfur dust. As dawn broke, they wedged a telephone coil against the cold rail to sample the induction current, frying their pocket synths in an explosion of blue sparks that ignited their final defiant chant: "El riel no duerme, nosotros tampoco."
